from data_new import X,t,CW
X("cannabis-flower","Cannabis flower (marijuana)","cann","Plant cannabis containing THC (the main intoxicant) and CBD (non-intoxicating).","Marijuana, weed, pot, grass, bud, herb; strains marketed as 'indica' or 'sativa'.",
"Cannabis has been used for at least 2,500 years; residues were found in burial braziers in western China. It entered Western medicine in the 1840s through William O'Shaughnessy, was effectively banned by the 1937 Marihuana Tax Act, removed from the US Pharmacopeia in 1942, and placed in Schedule I in 1970. California legalized medical use in 1996; Colorado and Washington legalized adult use in 2012.",
"Historically a tincture for pain, spasms and sleep. Today, state medical programs use it for chronic pain, chemotherapy nausea, and spasticity; FDA-approved cannabinoid medicines are dronabinol, nabilone, and Epidiolex (CBD).",
"Recreational use spread through jazz culture in the 1930s, the 1960s counterculture, and today's legal markets. Average THC in seized flower rose from about 4 percent in the 1990s to around 15 percent or more today (NIDA).",
"Federally Schedule I in the US (a 2024 proposal to move it to Schedule III was still under review). Legal for adults in many US states and in Canada, Uruguay, Germany (limited) and elsewhere; controlled under the UN 1961 Single Convention.",
["Smoked or vaped flower brings a quick, wave-like high: relaxed body, laughter, brighter music and food, and wandering, associative thinking. Strains feel different mainly because of THC strength and the THC-to-CBD balance, more than the 'indica/sativa' label.","Short-term memory and time sense go first: a sentence started can be forgotten mid-way, and minutes stretch. Red eyes, dry mouth and the 'munchies' are classic.","With strong flower, the same high can tip into racing heart, paranoia and a feeling of being stuck in a loop, especially for new users or people prone to anxiety or psychosis."],
["Inhaled, effects begin within minutes: a warm head rush, faster heartbeat, and lightness.","Over 10 to 15 minutes relaxation and laughter build; senses sharpen.","The peak, at about 15 to 30 minutes, brings the strongest time distortion, memory gaps and appetite.","The high plateaus for about an hour.","It fades over 2 to 3 hours into sleepiness.","Dry mouth, hunger, and a mild fog.","Some feel slow or foggy the next morning; daily users may feel irritable without it."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("seconds to minutes","5-15 min","15-30 min","30-60 min","1-3 h","2-4 h","next morning"),"oral":t("30-90 min","1-2 h","2-4 h","4-6 h","6-8 h","8-12 h","next day")},
{"inhaled-smoked":"Smoked in joints or pipes, or vaporized.","oral":"Eaten in edibles; see the edibles page."},
"Cannabis alone is not known to cause fatal overdose, but it causes panic, fainting, vomiting, psychosis, and impaired-driving crashes.",
"Dried flower is sticky, crumbly plant matter with a strong skunky smell. Legal-market flower is labeled with THC content; illicit products can be sprayed with synthetic cannabinoids."+CW,
"Inhaled THC peaks in the blood within minutes, then moves into fat.",
lohi="Stronger flower and repeated inhalation stack quickly; very high-THC flower makes the anxious, paranoid side more likely.",
src=["Ren M. et al. The origins of cannabis smoking: chemical residue evidence from the first millennium BCE in the Pamirs. Science Advances 2019","DEA proposed rule to reschedule marijuana (2024)"])

X("cannabis-concentrates","Cannabis concentrates (wax, shatter, dabs, vape oil)","cann","Extracts with very high THC content.","Dabs, wax, shatter, budder, rosin, live resin, hash oil, BHO, vape carts.",
"Hashish is centuries old; modern solvent-extracted concentrates spread in the 2010s with legal markets and 'dabbing'.","THC vape oil and concentrates are sold through some state medical programs.","Concentrates and vape cartridges are popular because they are discreet and very strong, often several times the THC of flower.",
"Same as cannabis flower. Illicit vape carts were linked to the 2019 EVALI lung-injury outbreak, caused largely by vitamin E acetate.",
["Concentrates hit fast and hard: a heavy rush to the head, pounding heart and a sudden, intense high that can overwhelm even experienced flower users.","The high is more 'pure THC' and can feel narrower and more disorienting, with stronger dizziness and time distortion.","Heavy daily concentrate use is linked in studies to higher risk of psychosis, cannabis use disorder, and cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome."],
["Inhaled concentrates take effect within seconds: a strong head rush and pounding heart.","Within minutes the high rises steeply; coughing is common.","The peak is intense and early: disorientation, heavy body, and for some, panic.","The plateau lasts about an hour.","Fades over 1 to 3 hours.","Heavy fatigue and hunger.","Daily users can feel foggy and irritable between uses."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("seconds","2-10 min","10-30 min","30-60 min","1-3 h","2-4 h","next morning")},{"inhaled-smoked":"Vaporized ('dabbed') or vaped from cartridges."},
"Concentrates cause more panic, fainting, vomiting and psychosis emergencies than flower; illicit vape carts can contain harmful additives.",
"Concentrates range from glassy brittle sheets (shatter) to soft wax or thick oil in cartridges."+CW,
"Concentrate THC reaches the brain within seconds of inhalation.",
lohi="Because concentrates are so strong, even a small amount moves people from mild to overwhelming effects.",
src=["CDC: Outbreak of Lung Injury Associated with E-cigarette or Vaping Products (2019-2020)","Di Forti M. et al. Lancet Psychiatry 2019 (high-potency cannabis and psychosis)"])

X("cannabis-edibles","Cannabis edibles","cann","Foods, candies and drinks containing THC.","Gummies, chocolates, brownies, cookies, beverages, tinctures.",
"Edible cannabis, such as bhang in India, is ancient. Modern commercial edibles grew with legalization; Colorado added labeling and serving rules after early emergency visits in 2014.",
"Oral THC is the route of dronabinol and many medical-market products for sleep, pain and nausea.","Edibles are popular because there is no smoke, but they cause the most accidental overdoses, especially in children and new users.",
"Same as cannabis; legal markets require child-resistant packaging and labels, but illicit edibles often copy candy brands.",
["Edibles feel different from smoked cannabis: slow to start, heavier and more body-centered, with a stronger 'stoned', dreamy feeling because the liver makes 11-hydroxy-THC.","The delay is the main danger: people think nothing is happening and take more, then everything arrives at once.","An edible high can last most of a day, with confusion, vomiting, panic, or hallucinations at high levels. Children who eat edibles can become very drowsy or have seizures."],
["Effects usually begin 30 minutes to 2 hours after eating, slower on a full stomach.","The rise is gradual: body heaviness, warmth and giddiness.","The peak, around 2 to 4 hours, is the strongest and longest-feeling high, sometimes with time loss and confusion.","The plateau can last for hours.","It fades slowly over 6 to 8 hours or more.","Sleepiness and lingering fog.","Hangover-like grogginess often continues into the next day."],
{"oral":t("30 min-2 h","1-3 h","2-4 h","4-6 h","6-12 h","12-24 h","next day")},{"oral":"Eaten or drunk."},
"Edibles are a leading cause of cannabis emergency visits and child poisonings; call Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) if a child eats one.",
"Edibles look exactly like normal candy or baked goods, which is why they must be locked away from children and pets."+CW,
"Swallowed THC is converted by the liver to 11-hydroxy-THC, which is more potent.",
lohi="Taking more before the first has peaked is the classic edible mistake; effects can then become overwhelming for many hours.",
src=["Wang GS. et al. Pediatric marijuana exposures in Colorado. JAMA Pediatrics 2016"])

X("delta-8-thc","Delta-8-THC, THC-O and HHC (hemp-derived cannabinoids)","cann","Semi-synthetic or converted cannabinoids marketed from hemp.","Delta-8, D8, THC-O acetate, HHC, THCP; sold as gummies, vapes and 'legal weed'.",
"After the 2018 US Farm Bill legalized hemp with low delta-9-THC, companies began chemically converting hemp CBD into delta-8-THC, and later HHC and THC-O, selling them in gas stations and online.",
"None are approved medicines.","Marketed as a legal or 'lighter' high; FDA and poison centers report rising exposures, many in children.",
"A legal gray area. The DEA stated in 2023 that THC-O is a controlled substance; many states ban delta-8 and HHC. A 2025 federal law redefined hemp to close this loophole, effective in late 2026.",
["Delta-8 feels like a milder, clearer version of THC; HHC sits between delta-8 and delta-9; THC-O is described as slow and heavy. All act on the same CB1 receptor.","The main concern is manufacturing: products are made by chemical reactions in unregulated labs and may contain leftover acids, solvents, metals or unknown by-products.","Labels are often wrong, so a product sold as delta-8 may contain delta-9-THC or synthetic cannabinoids."],
["Inhaled effects begin within minutes; edibles take 30 minutes to 2 hours.","A gradual relaxation and lightness.","The peak brings typical cannabis effects: time distortion, appetite and sleepiness, with possible anxiety.","Plateau of an hour (vapes) to several hours (edibles).","Gradual fade.","Dry mouth and grogginess.","Possible next-day fog, particularly with edibles."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("minutes","5-15 min","15-45 min","1 h","2-3 h","3-5 h","next morning"),"oral":t("30 min-2 h","1-3 h","2-4 h","4-6 h","6-10 h","12-24 h","next day")},
{"inhaled-smoked":"Vapes.","oral":"Gummies and edibles."},"FDA and poison centers report vomiting, hallucinations, confusion and loss of consciousness, often in children.",
"Often sold as brightly colored gummies or disposable vape pens."+CW,"These compounds act at CB1 like THC.",
lohi="Strength varies from batch to batch, so the jump from mild to overwhelming is unpredictable.",
src=["FDA: 5 Things to Know about Delta-8 THC (2022)","DEA letter on THC-O acetate (2023)"])

X("synthetic-cannabinoids","Synthetic cannabinoids (K2, Spice)","cann","Lab-made chemicals sprayed on plant material that strongly activate cannabinoid receptors.","K2, Spice, 'fake weed', 'zombie'; chemicals such as JWH-018, AB-FUBINACA, 5F-ADB, MDMB-4en-PINACA.",
"Many were created by researchers, notably John W. Huffman (the 'JWH' series) in the 1990s, to study cannabinoid receptors. From about 2004 they appeared in smoking blends; each time one was banned, another replaced it.",
"None have medical use.","Popular because they were legal, cheap, and invisible on standard drug tests; widely used in prisons and among people experiencing homelessness. Mass poisonings included the 2016 'zombie' outbreak in Brooklyn and 2018 batches contaminated with brodifacoum (rat poison) that caused severe bleeding.",
"Many are Schedule I in the US (including under the Synthetic Drug Abuse Prevention Act of 2012), and new ones are continually scheduled. Many are controlled under the UN 1971 Convention.",
["Synthetic cannabinoids are full agonists at CB1, unlike THC, which is partial, so effects are far stronger and less predictable: sudden dissociation, intense anxiety, and 'zombie-like' unresponsiveness.","People describe an abrupt, short, heavy high followed by fast craving. Many find it frightening.","Serious harms include seizures, psychosis, violent agitation, kidney injury, heart attack, stroke and death; daily users get severe withdrawal."],
["Effects begin within seconds to minutes of smoking.","The rise is fast and heavy, with a racing heart.","The peak can include confusion, paranoia, vomiting, or unresponsiveness.","Effects are relatively short, often 1 to 2 hours.","Comedown brings anxiety and craving.","Heavy fatigue; for some, lingering psychosis.","Daily users experience withdrawal: sweating, vomiting, insomnia, irritability."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("seconds-minutes","2-10 min","10-30 min","30-90 min","1-3 h","3-6 h","next day")},{"inhaled-smoked":"Smoked or vaped; also sprayed on paper in prisons."},
"Synthetic cannabinoids cause seizures, kidney injury, heart attacks, strokes, psychosis and deaths; there is no antidote.",
"Usually dried plant material in shiny packets labeled 'not for human consumption', or liquids for vapes. The chemical and strength vary from batch to batch, even within a packet."+CW,
"Most are full CB1 agonists far stronger than THC.",lohi="Because the chemicals are so potent and unevenly sprayed, two puffs from the same packet can differ dramatically.",
src=["Adams AJ. et al. 'Zombie' outbreak caused by AMB-FUBINACA. NEJM 2017","CDC: Outbreak of coagulopathy associated with synthetic cannabinoid use (2018)"])

X("dronabinol-nabilone","Dronabinol and nabilone (prescription THC)","cann","Prescription synthetic THC (dronabinol) and a THC analog (nabilone).","Marinol, Syndros (dronabinol); Cesamet (nabilone).",
"Dronabinol was approved in 1985 for chemotherapy nausea and in 1992 for AIDS-related appetite loss; nabilone was approved in 1985.","Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting; appetite loss in AIDS (dronabinol).","Little recreational use; effects are slow, like a strong edible.","Marinol and generic dronabinol capsules are Schedule III; Syndros and nabilone are Schedule II.",
["Prescription THC feels like an edible: slow onset, sleepy, dreamy, with appetite stimulation.","Some patients feel anxious, dizzy or 'too high', especially older adults.","Its effects are more predictable than street cannabis because the content is known."],
["Onset in about 30 minutes to 1 hour.","Gradual relaxation and appetite.","Peak around 2 to 4 hours.","Plateau several hours.","Fades over 4 to 6 hours or longer.","Drowsiness.","Possible next-day fog."],
{"oral":t("30-60 min","1-2 h","2-4 h","4-6 h","6-12 h","12-24 h","next day")},{"oral":"Capsules (Marinol, Cesamet) or oral solution (Syndros)."},
"Too much causes anxiety, dizziness, fast heartbeat and confusion; serious harm is rare in adults.",
"Marinol is a round soft gelatin capsule; Cesamet is a capsule; Syndros is a liquid.","Swallowed THC is converted to 11-hydroxy-THC.",
src=["DailyMed: Marinol, Syndros, Cesamet labels"])
